<PaperLayout
  title="You CAN Teach an Old Model New Tricks"
  subtitle="Domain Adaptation via Complementary Subspace Expansion"
  venue="NeurIPS 2026"
  authors={authors}
  links={links}
  description="CoSE adapts a deployed vision-language embedding model to a new domain without touching its legacy index: the frozen embedding is preserved by construction and a jointly trained LoRA side channel is appended alongside it."
>
  <section>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cose/img/bc_frontier.png"
        alt="Preservation-constrained backward-compatibility frontier on four domains; CoSE is the best full-preservation point on each"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Preservation-constrained BC frontier under a 5 pp budget. <em>x</em>: domain R@1
        gain over the frozen model on a migration-compatible index; <em>y</em>: how many of
        &#123;Flickr, COCO, ImageNet&#125; stay within 5 pp of frozen. Circled: the best
        exact-BC method at 3/3 preservation — CoSE on all four domains. The red segment
        connects CoSE at α = 0.5 and at the val-tuned α*, both from one checkpoint.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Production retrieval requires three properties jointly: domain adaptation,
      general-retrieval preservation, and backward compatibility with deployed indices.
      Existing methods achieve at most two: PEFT breaks the index and degrades general
      retrieval; BCT preserves the index only approximately; L2C preserves general retrieval
      but still modifies the deployed embedding. We introduce <strong>CoSE</strong>
      (Complementary Subspace Expansion), a side-branch LoRA whose frozen pathway is
      structurally untouched: legacy <em>z</em><sub>old</sub> entries are never rewritten,
      and a jointly-trained <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> channel is appended alongside them. At
      α = 1 the deployed score recovers the frozen ranking exactly; for α &lt; 1, domain
      gains require computing <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> per document but the legacy index
      stays valid throughout migration. A three-term joint loss converts the frozen+LoRA
      concatenation into Pareto-efficient retrieval. Across four domains (Korean broadcast,
      medical radiology, art paintings, remote sensing) and 3 seeds, CoSE at 6.4M trainable
      parameters stays within ±0.048 of frozen on Flickr/COCO/ImageNet at α = 0.5 and is
      domain-competitive at val-tuned α* — the only configuration we evaluate that
      simultaneously delivers structural BC, general retention, and competitive domain
      quality from a single checkpoint.
    </p>
  </section>

