  <section>
    <h2>1. Why expansion is necessary</h2>
    <p>
      Two short results bound the design space. First, exact compatibility
      <em>within</em> the deployed <em>d</em>-dimensional channel is trivial: if an adapted
      query encoder reproduces every score against a full-rank legacy index, it must equal
      the frozen encoder. Any non-trivial adaptation that keeps legacy scores exact has to
      <strong>expand the score channel</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second, a direct-sum normal form: any expanded representation from which the frozen
      embedding can be read out by a fixed linear projection is, up to a change of
      coordinates, <code>[ z<sub>old</sub> ; h(x) ]</code>. CoSE's concatenation is
      therefore not an ad hoc trick — it is the canonical shape of auditable backward
      compatibility.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Side-branch LoRA architecture</h2>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cose/img/architecture.png"
        alt="CoSE architecture: a frozen pass produces z_old, a LoRA side branch produces z_new, and they are concatenated"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Pass 1 (frozen) runs the unmodified base encoder and produces the preserved legacy
        embedding <em>z</em><sub>old</sub>. Pass 2 is a trainable side branch with LoRA on the
        attention projections that consumes detached Pass-1 hidden states and produces the
        complementary <em>z</em><sub>new</sub>. The retrieval embedding is
        <em>z</em><sub>final</sub> = [<em>z</em><sub>old</sub> ‖ <em>z</em><sub>new</sub>].
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Because the first <em>d</em> coordinates are produced by a forward pass identical to
      the base encoder, every score against a legacy document is exactly the frozen-model
      score (index preservation by construction). On Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B
      (<em>L</em> = 28, <em>d</em> = 2048, rank 16) the side branch has
      <strong>6.4M</strong> trainable parameters, 0.30% of the base.
    </p>
    <p>
      At inference, a single scalar blends the two channels:
    </p>
    <pre class="citation">s_α(q, d) = α · ⟨z_old(q), z_old(d)⟩ + (1 − α) · ⟨z_new(q), z_new(d)⟩</pre>
    <p>
      For every α ∈ [0, 1] this is a positive-semidefinite kernel, so α indexes a family of
      valid retrieval kernels rather than a heuristic interpolation. α = 1 is the frozen
      model exactly; α = 0.5 is the unweighted concatenation and a zero-tune default; an
      operator can pick α* with a 1-D sweep on a small validation set — no retraining.
    </p>
    <p>
      Training uses three terms, each anchoring one operating point:
      InfoNCE on <em>z</em><sub>final</sub> (the α = 0.5 blend), InfoNCE on
      <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> alone (the α = 0 endpoint), and a Matryoshka loss so
      <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> can be truncated for cheaper storage. The α = 1 endpoint is
      anchored for free by the frozen pretraining.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Results across four domains</h2>
    <p>
      We adapt Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B to Korean broadcast/cultural imagery (HAN extended),
      medical radiology (ROCO), art paintings (SemArt) and remote sensing (RSICD), and track
      general retrieval on Flickr30K, COCO and ImageNet zero-shot. All learned methods use
      6.4M trainable parameters; numbers are 3-seed means on the test split.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
