    <p class="table-caption">
      Domain R@1 is avg(i2t, t2i). Migration R@1 queries with the adapted encoder against a
      legacy frozen index (for CoSE, the expanded index, with α = 1 guaranteeing exact frozen
      recovery). General columns are ImageNet zero-shot top-1 and COCO/Flickr avg R@1.
      Selected baselines shown; the paper also reports BCT-cosine and a parameter-matched L2C
      on every domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      CoSE is <strong>domain-competitive, not domain-SOTA</strong>: at α* it trails the
      per-domain leader by 0.012–0.027 on three domains (within noise) and by 0.072 on ROCO.
      What it buys is a distinct frontier point. LoRA's domain peaks cost up to 0.363
      ImageNet top-1; CoSE at α = 0.5 stays within ±0.048 of frozen on every general cell.
      And for shifted single-encoder baselines, querying a legacy index captures only 27–61%
      of their self-gain, because query and corpus encoders drift apart — CoSE's migration
      column equals its domain column by construction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. One checkpoint, a whole operating curve</h2>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cose/img/alpha_sweep.png"
        alt="Domain validation R@1 and ImageNet top-1 as a function of alpha on four domains"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Sweeping α on a single CoSE checkpoint. Red: domain val i2t R@1 (left axis). Blue:
        ImageNet top-1 (right axis). α* sits consistently below 0.5 — domain signal
        dominates — with a val→test gap of at most 0.045.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      LoRA, BCT and L2C each produce one point; CoSE exposes the full curve at deployment
      time. The same checkpoint also supports <strong>partial branching</strong> (sharing
      the first <em>K</em> layers cuts forward overhead from 2× to about 1.5× at
      <em>K</em> = <em>L</em>/2 for ≤ 0.04 R@1 on three of four domains) and
      <strong>Matryoshka truncation</strong> of <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> for cheaper storage.
      The legacy <em>z</em><sub>old</sub> slice is preserved in every variant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. Why train jointly?</h2>
    <p>
      A natural baseline is to ensemble the frozen model's score with a separately trained
      LoRA. It does not work as a substitute: the val-tuned ensemble weight collapses to
      α ≤ 0.05 on three of four domains, essentially recovering LoRA along with its general
      drift. A standard LoRA is a low-rank perturbation of the base (cos ≈ 0.85), so the two
      branches make the same mistakes and averaging is redundant.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cose/img/pareto_ensemble.png"
        alt="Alpha-swept Pareto curves of CoSE versus a Frozen+LoRA score ensemble on four domains"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        α-swept trade-off between domain R@1 and general R@1 for CoSE (red) and a
        Frozen+LoRA score ensemble (blue). Diamonds mark each method's val-selected α*.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The standalone InfoNCE on <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> is what makes CoSE different. It
      forces the side channel to be retrieval-valid on its own, which emergently places
      <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> at cos ≈ 0.5 to <em>z</em><sub>old</sub> — partially
      decorrelated, so blending behaves like an ensemble. Removing that term (HAN extended,
      seed 0) collapses α = 0.1 retrieval from 0.489 to 0.185 and z<sub>old</sub>/z<sub>new</sub>
      overlap@1 from 26.0% to 2.4%.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Takeaway</h2>
    <p>
      When existing indices are large and re-indexing is costly, CoSE offers a
      backward-compatible route to domain adaptation that keeps general retrieval intact.
      The base encoder is never modified, α = 1 recovers the legacy ranking exactly, and
      α &lt; 1 adds a complementary domain score without rewriting old coordinates — so
      migration becomes a gradual α-sweep instead of a hard cutover.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Scope.</strong> CoSE does not eliminate re-embedding: for α &lt; 1,
      <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> must still be computed per document. Full branching costs 2×
      forward compute and 2× embedding width, reducible but not to zero. Each run
      specializes <em>z</em><sub>new</sub> to one domain, and all experiments use
      Qwen3-VL-Embedding as the base encoder. Where latency, storage, or pure peak-domain
      accuracy dominate, single-embedding methods are cheaper.
    </p>
  </section>

